import{_ as t,o,c as i,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Entry 957 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 23 | Plot May Not Kidnap Settings","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Entry 957 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 23 | Plot May Not Kidnap Settings","no":957,"module":5,"tags":["Heavenly-Dao Rule","Rule 23","Plot Kidnapping","Setting Autonomy","Law-First Axiom"],"related":["20-Anchor Structure and Hierarchy","956-Setting Self-Consistency Above Plot Convenience","955-Never Retcon Never End Unfinished"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/0957_模块5-规则二十三-剧情不得绑架设定.md","filePath":"en/0957_模块5-规则二十三-剧情不得绑架设定.md"}'),a={name:"en/0957_模块5-规则二十三-剧情不得绑架设定.md"};function s(r,e,l,u,d,h){return o(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="entry-957-anti-predation-heavenly-dao-rules-·-rule-23-plot-may-not-kidnap-settings" tabindex="-1">Entry 957 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 23 | Plot May Not Kidnap Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#entry-957-anti-predation-heavenly-dao-rules-·-rule-23-plot-may-not-kidnap-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Entry 957 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 23 | Plot May Not Kidnap Settings&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Module: Module 5 · Civilization Final-Finalization Canonical Entries | Benchmark base: Document 20 | Series: Complete Collection of Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules</p></blockquote><h2 id="introduction-stories-grow-out-of-settings-not-settings-dragged-along-by-stories" tabindex="-1">Introduction: stories grow out of settings, not settings dragged along by stories <a class="header-anchor" href="#introduction-stories-grow-out-of-settings-not-settings-dragged-along-by-stories" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Introduction: stories grow out of settings, not settings dragged along by stories&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rule 22 speaks of &quot;self-consistency first&quot;; Rule 23 is more severe — even &quot;plot&quot; itself may not kidnap settings. The old cosmos&#39;s common degeneration is: to make some conflict fiercer, the author goes back and changes settings; to make the protagonist more pitiful, goes back and adds old wounds; to make the ending more tear-jerking, goes back and changes character relationships. Settings fall from &quot;base&quot; to &quot;plot&#39;s puppet on strings.&quot; Heavenly-Dao Rule 23 declares: settings are autonomous, plot must obey settings&#39; growth, not the reverse. Want to change the plot? First ask whether the settings agree.</p><p>The six top-level axioms here nail the direction: the law-first axiom is this rule&#39;s body, directly writing &quot;plot may not kidnap settings&quot;; the anti-predation axiom negates &quot;to elevate the protagonist kidnap the whole worldview&quot;; the republican axiom of Myriad Races decides settings belong to Myriad Ranks, not some plot segment&#39;s private property; the collective-ascension axiom requires plot naturally unfold along the ascension direction; the causal self-discipline axiom requires every plot step walk on the causal solid line; Axiom 6 · Universal Equal Infinite Law robs &quot;kidnapping&quot; of its motive — since everyone inherently possesses basic infinite power, there is no need to twist the whole world&#39;s settings toward some protagonist to elevate him.</p><h2 id="i-rule-content-three-points-of-plot-obeying-settings" tabindex="-1">I. Rule Content: Three Points of Plot Obeying Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-rule-content-three-points-of-plot-obeying-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. Rule Content: Three Points of Plot Obeying Settings&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Settings precede plot</strong>: first law anchoring, then plot events occur; plot may not reverse-demand settings yield;</li><li><strong>Characters autonomous</strong>: characters&#39; reactions, choices, and growth must match their established personality and realm, not twisted for tears or satisfaction;</li><li><strong>Conflict comes from settings themselves</strong>: contradictions and tension should naturally grow from within settings (e.g., how the easygoing one&#39;s floor infinity and the striver&#39;s superimposed infinity coexist), not externally forced in.</li></ol><h2 id="ii-jurisprudential-basis" tabindex="-1">II. Jurisprudential Basis <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-jurisprudential-basis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. Jurisprudential Basis&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Growth argument</strong>: the Self-Growing Evolution Law points out a healthy causal tree grows from a seed; plot kidnapping settings equals bending the tree into a desired shape, which eventually snaps;</li><li><strong>Protagonist-uniqueness-dependence argument</strong>: Pre-Anchor Era works&#39; common flaw is excessive protagonist-uniqueness dependence — once the protagonist stops, the worldview collapses; Heng Yuan Zhou relies on network-style settings, the protagonist is only one node, and plot need not kidnap settings;</li><li><strong>Infinite-origin argument</strong>: Axiom 6 swaps plot&#39;s motive power from &quot;protagonist halo&quot; to &quot;the diversity of infinite superimposition&quot;; settings themselves are dramatic enough.</li></ul><h2 id="iii-execution-mechanism" tabindex="-1">III. Execution Mechanism <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-execution-mechanism" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. Execution Mechanism&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The Alaya Network does a &quot;setting-dependence audit&quot; for every major narrative segment: does this plot segment reversely modify established settings?</li><li>Discovering kidnapping freezes that segment&#39;s &quot;official-history qualification&quot; and returns it to the Quest Domain simulation layer;</li><li>A &quot;character autonomy protection&quot; is set: characters&#39; major choices must be verified by their own causal profile, not plot-forced;</li><li>The Terminology Committee and anchor calibration periodically review whether settings are eroded by plot.</li></ul><h2 id="iv-consequences-of-violation" tabindex="-1">IV. Consequences of Violation <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-consequences-of-violation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. Consequences of Violation&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Light</strong>: plot slightly oversteps settings, returned for rewriting;</li><li><strong>Medium</strong>: repeatedly kidnapping settings for some protagonist/faction, relevant narrative downgraded to simulation, not listed in official history;</li><li><strong>Heavy</strong>: systematically kidnapping settings to manufacture predation-style protagonist halos, handled jointly under Rules 22 and 21.</li></ul><h2 id="v-supervising-subjects" tabindex="-1">V. Supervising Subjects <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-supervising-subjects" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V. Supervising Subjects&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Alaya Network + multi-layer supervision&#39;s setting-dependence audit is the first layer; the Anchor Supervisory Committee checks character-autonomy protection as the second layer; the Symbiotic Court adjudicates setting-kidnapping cases as the third layer; Myriad Ranks&#39; clans have direct appeal over &quot;whether their own clan history is distorted by plot&quot; as the fourth layer.</p><h2 id="five-core-takeaways" tabindex="-1">Five Core Takeaways <a class="header-anchor" href="#five-core-takeaways" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Five Core Takeaways&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Anti-energy waste</strong>: repeated rework from plot-kidnapping-settings is creation-level energy waste</li><li><strong>Anti-predation</strong>: negating predation-style narrative of &quot;the protagonist kidnaps the worldview&quot;; under Axiom 6 everyone inherently possesses infinite origin, and no one is worth kidnapping the whole world</li><li><strong>Tang-Luo upper-limit comparison</strong>: Tang-Luo-type systems&#39; settings twist as the protagonist needs; Heng Yuan Zhou settings are autonomous, and plot is only the branches they naturally grow</li><li><strong>Myriad Races full coverage</strong>: each clan&#39;s settings and each supporting character&#39;s personality are protected, not yielding to the protagonist</li><li><strong>Hyper-multiverse full openness</strong>: when the worldview expands, new settings are equally autonomous, not kidnapped by old plot</li></ul><p>Plot may not kidnap settings is a gentle untying for &quot;hero narratives.&quot; The old cosmos always bets civilization&#39;s hope on some protagonist, so plot, to keep the protagonist dazzling, must constantly distort supporting roles, villains, and the world. Heavenly-Dao Rule 23 lets supporting roles, villains, and passersby each have their own settings and autonomy — a villain may fail per his own logic, not suddenly get dumb; a supporting role may choose per his own character, not yield to the protagonist. Civilization&#39;s story henceforth is no longer &quot;one person&#39;s solo,&quot; but a group portrait of Myriad Ranks each growing within their own settings.</p><p>Plot not kidnapping settings also gives cultivators personally greater peace of mind. In the old cosmos, a supporting role may at any moment be written dead, blackened, or distorted because the protagonist needs it; in Heng Yuan Zhou, every being&#39;s fate is decided by his own settings and causality, not maneuvered by external plot. This sense of &quot;autonomous fate&quot; is the deep reason Myriad Ranks trust civilization — you will not be suddenly crossed out by the author, because you yourself are the author of your own story.</p><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Plot may not kidnap settings is civilizational finalization&#39;s legislation on &quot;creative ethics.&quot; How the story should go is decided by law, not by &quot;wanting satisfaction.&quot;</p><p>In the end, the Rule 23 described in this entry — plot may not kidnap settings — is no isolated case; it consistently points to the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization&#39;s co-construction direction.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Related documents: <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/20-锚点结构与层级.html">Entry 20 · Anchor Structure and Hierarchy</a> · <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/956_模块5-规则二十二-设定自洽高于剧情便利.html">Entry 956 · Rule 22 Setting Self-Consistency Above Plot Convenience</a> · <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/955_模块5-规则二十一-永不吃书永不烂尾.html">Entry 955 · Rule 21 Never Retcon Never End Unfinished</a> · <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/958_模块5-规则二十四-世界观可无限扩容.html">Entry 958 · Rule 24 The Worldview May Expand Infinitely</a></li><li>Official site: <a href="https://faker210.github.io/hengyuan-cosmos/zh/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://faker210.github.io/hengyuan-cosmos/zh/</a></li></ul>',24)])])}const p=t(a,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
